# Append-only files merge by union rather than by conflict.
#
# ## The problem this removes
#
# Every PR adds a dated entry to the top of CHANGELOG.md, because CLAUDE.md
# asks it to. Two open PRs therefore always edit the same region of the same
# file, so the second one to be merged conflicts every single time, on a file
# where the correct resolution is never in doubt: keep both entries.
#
# That is tedious on its own and it is not the real cost. A conflicting PR is
# not merely inconvenient here, it is dangerous, because GitHub runs
# `pull_request` workflows against the computed merge commit: a CONFLICTING PR
# triggers nothing at all. No queued run, no failed run, and the checks still
# shown on the page are whatever ran on an older head, all green and all
# describing code that is no longer there. docs/OPERATIONS.md records an hour
# lost to reading that as an Actions outage. So the guaranteed conflict on a
# file nobody disagrees about was silently switching off every quality gate in
# the repository, on every second PR, until somebody noticed and merged main
# in by hand.
#
# `union` is a built-in git merge driver: on a conflicting hunk it keeps the
# lines from both sides instead of writing markers. For a file that is only
# ever prepended to, that is exactly the resolution a person would make.
#
# ## What this does and does not buy, measured rather than assumed
#
# VERIFIED: a local merge of the base branch into a PR branch resolves this
# file with no markers and both entries kept. That is the step the protocol
# already tells you to take, so the hand-resolution is gone.
#
# NOT VERIFIED, and do not read this file as promising it: whether GitHub's
# own mergeability computation honors a merge driver from `.gitattributes`.
# It is computed on GitHub's servers, not by your git, and the widely reported
# behavior is that it does NOT. If that holds, a second PR touching this file
# is still shown as CONFLICTING and its `pull_request` workflows still do not
# run, which is the dangerous half.
#
# So this is a convenience with a possible safety benefit, and the actual
# protections against a stale-green PR are elsewhere and do not depend on it:
# `npm run pr:status` reads the real state rather than the checkmarks, and
# `.github/workflows/main-guard.yml` checks `main` after every push. Step 5 of
# the protocol in docs/OPERATIONS.md still stands: merge the base branch in.
#
# ## What union costs, stated rather than discovered
#
# A union merge never reports a conflict, so if two branches edit the SAME
# existing line, both versions survive and nothing asks. That is the wrong
# answer for source code and an acceptable one for a log of dated entries,
# where an edit to an already-published entry is rare and a duplicate is
# visible in review rather than silently wrong at runtime.
#
# This is why the list below is short and stays short. A file belongs here
# only when it is append-only by construction and its merge has one obvious
# answer. Source files, config and anything a guard reads do NOT belong here:
# for those a conflict is information, and silencing it would hide a real
# disagreement about behavior.
CHANGELOG.md merge=union
